,8,8,8,0,0,3,3,3,0],[0,4,0,4,4,0,0,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,3,0,0,4,4,0,4,0],[4,0,0,4,4,4,0,0,3,3,0,3,3,0,3,3,0,0,4,4,4,0,0,4],[0,4,4,0,4,4,3,3,0,0,3,3,3,3,0,0,3,3,4,4,0,4,4,0],[4,4,0,4,0,0,3,3,0,0,3,3,3,3,0,0,3,3,0,0,4,0,4,4],[4,4,4,4,0,4,3,3,3,3,0,3,3,0,3,3,3,3,4,0,4,4,4,4],[4,4,4,0,4,0,0,3,3,3,0,0,0,0,3,3,3,0,0,4,0,4,4,4]],"output":[[8,8,8,8,8],[0,0,8,8,0],[0,8,0,0,8],[8,8,8,8,8],[8,0,8,8,0]]}]},"teaching_variant":"canonical","teacher_models":["claude-opus-5-5"],"explanation":"The input is a 24×24 picture that is symmetric in eight ways at once. It is mirrored left–right, mirrored up–down, and mirrored across the diagonal. The fact sheet confirms all of these hold together for pairs 1 and 3 and for the test. For pair 2 it gives no symmetry numbers, so I checked one example by hand.\n\nIn every grid, a solid 5×5 block of colour 1 has been painted over part of the picture. The output is always 5×5, exactly the size of that block. So the output is the hidden content underneath the patch.\n\n**Checking pair 2.** The patch covers rows 0–4 and columns 18–22. Mirroring left–right sends column x to column 23−x, so the partner area is columns 5 down to 1 in the same rows.\n- Row 0, read from column 5 down to column 1, is `0 3 3 3 3`.\n- That is exactly the first row of the expected output.\n\n**The rule.** For each cell under the patch, try its seven symmetric partners in turn:\n- (y, 23−x), (23−y, x), (23−y, 23−x)\n- (x, y), (x, 23−y), (23−x, y), (23−x, 23−y)\n\nTake the first partner that is not colour 1, which skips partners that are damaged themselves. After that, crop the patch's bounding box.\n\n**Applying it to the test.** The patch sits at rows 6–10, columns 9–13. Its left–right mirror (columns 14 down to 10) partly overlaps the patch itself. In those cells the left–right partner is still colour 1, so the rule falls through to the next partner, the up–down mirror. That mirror lands in rows 13–17, which are clean, so every cell gets a value.\n\n**Teaching stages.**\n1. The input is the starting canvas.\n2. The full grid with the patch repaired.\n3. The cropped 5×5 answer.","code":"def transform(grid):\n    g = np.array(grid)\n    H, W = g.shape\n    patch = 1\n    ys, xs = np.where(g == patch)\n    y0, y1, x0, x1 = ys.min(), ys.max(), xs.min(), xs.max()\n    rep = g.copy()\n    for y, x in zip(ys, xs):\n        cands = [(y, W-1-x), (H-1-y, x), (H-1-y, W-1-x),\n                 (x, y), (x, H-1-y), (W-1-x, y), (W-1-x, H-1-y)]\n        for py, px in cands:\n            if 0 <= py < H and 0 <= px < W and g[py, px] != patch:\n                rep[y, x] = g[py, px]\n                break\n    emit(rep, \"Restore every cell hidden under the colour-1 patch from its first undamaged symmetric partner (mirror left-right, up-down, or transpose).\")\n    out = rep[y0:y1+1, x0:x1+1].copy()\n    emit(out, \"Crop the repaired region where the patch was: this is the answer.\")\n    return out\n","candidate_tactics":{"rigid":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"The input is a 24×24 picture that is symmetric in eight ways at once."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"It is mirrored left–right, mirrored up–down, and mirrored across the diagonal."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"Mirroring left–right sends column x to column 23−x, so the partner area is columns 5 down to 1 in the same rows."}],"repair":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Take the first partner that is not colour 1, which skips partners that are damaged themselves."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Restore every cell hidden under the colour-1 patch from its first undamaged symmetric partner (mirror left-right, up-down, or transpose)."}],"dynamics":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"In those cells the left–right partner is still colour 1, so the rule falls through to the next partner, the up–down mirror."}],"crop":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"After that, crop the patch's bounding box."},{"source":"final explanation","text":"The cropped 5×5 answer."},{"source":"verified program checkpoint","text":"Crop the repaired region where the patch was: this is the answer."}],"layers":[{"source":"final explanation","text":"Its left–right mirror (columns 14 down to 10) partly overlaps the patch itself."}]},"provenance":{"reference_assisted":false,"query_labels_used_for_rule_diagnosis":false},"artifact_sha256":"aa1b279668ee7338323333ccd206664c096b46a41b82770b42ba7e65d0ca236a","task_sha256":"779f160fde2d4e5d7d25824ed7170ada125e308392f920c1c0d7195e2cbdf742","validation":"Frozen corpus recorded successful replay on teaching fixtures; synthetic generalization not established."}
